course: dictionary
lesson: 6256
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. The word mole is a good example. Read the words around it, because they are its clues. «But real moles do not spring-clean with whitewash» is about an animal that lives underground.
explain: In a spy story, «the mole» means someone who works secretly inside a group and passes on its secrets. The story tells you this, not the animal. Ask: which meaning fits the words around it? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: A mole can also be a small dark spot on someone's skin, or a stone wall built out into the sea to protect a harbour. The clues decide which one you mean. A mole that digs tunnels is nothing like a mole on your arm, so always read on before you choose.
fact: Mole has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one is meant.
fact: A mole can be a small furry animal with tiny eyes and big front paws that digs tunnels and eats worms.
fact: A mole can also be a small dark spot on someone's skin; if it changes shape, colour or size, show a doctor.
fact: A mole can be a spy who works secretly inside an organisation and passes on its secrets.
fact: In science, a mole is a unit for counting a huge number of tiny particles, used by older students.
exercise: «But real moles do not spring-clean with whitewash.» In this sentence, what is a mole? | A dark spot on the skin | A spy who works secretly inside a group | *A small animal that digs tunnels | A stone wall built out into the sea
exercise: Ella's doctor said to watch her mole, because it had changed in size. What is a mole here? | A spy inside a group | A wall to protect a harbour | *A small dark spot on the skin | A unit for counting tiny particles
exercise: In the spy story, the mole passed the army's secret plans to the enemy. What does mole mean here? | *A spy who works secretly inside a group | A small animal that digs tunnels | A small dark spot on the skin | A stone wall in the sea
test: «The mole kept the big waves away from the boats in the harbour.» What is a mole here? | A small furry animal | *A stone wall built out into the sea | A small dark spot on the skin | A spy inside a group
test: Sam's science teacher says one mole contains a huge number of tiny particles. What is a mole in science? | *A unit for counting a huge number of tiny particles | A small animal that digs tunnels | A dark spot on someone's skin | A spy who passes on secrets
exam: «The mole burrowed under the lawn and left a molehill.» What does mole mean in this sentence? | A stone wall built out into the sea | *A small furry animal that digs tunnels | A unit for counting tiny particles | A spy who works secretly inside a group
